BEGIN:VCALENDAR
VERSION:2.0
PRODID:-//4AM Wisdom//Three Week Trial//EN
CALSCALE:GREGORIAN
METHOD:PUBLISH
X-WR-CALNAME:4AM Wisdom
X-WR-TIMEZONE:America/New_York
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260901@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260831T170000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260901T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260901T011200
SUMMARY:Silence is not empty
DESCRIPTION:John Cage\nIdea: Silence is not the absence of experience. Attention reveals events we habitually exclude as noise or nothing.\nContext: Cage's work on silence and listening; especially 4′33″ and his writings on sound.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260902@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260831T170000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260902T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260902T011200
SUMMARY:Can a game stay open?
DESCRIPTION:James P. Carse\nIdea: A finite game is played to conclude in victory. An infinite game is played so that play can continue. Ask what your present activity is trying to preserve: victory or possibility.\nSource: Finite and Infinite Games.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260903@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260831T170000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260903T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260903T011200
SUMMARY:Wholeness has degrees
DESCRIPTION:Christopher Alexander\nIdea: Places and things are not simply whole or fragmented. Their structure can intensify or diminish the life of the larger field around them.\nContext: Alexander's later work on centers and degrees of life; The Nature of Order.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260904@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260831T170000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260904T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260904T011200
SUMMARY:Hope contradicts now
DESCRIPTION:Jürgen Moltmann\nIdea: Hope is not merely confidence that present trends will turn out well. It can contradict the apparent finality of the present by orienting life toward what is promised but not yet actual.\nContext: Theology of Hope.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260905@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260831T170000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260905T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260905T011200
SUMMARY:The margin may be center
DESCRIPTION:Flannery O’Connor\nIdea: The grotesque can disclose realities that ordinary proportion conceals. Distortion is sometimes a way of making an invisible moral structure visible.\nContext: O'Connor's fiction and essays on the grotesque and the Christian novelist.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260906@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260831T170000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260906T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260906T011200
SUMMARY:What resists your map?
DESCRIPTION:Marshall McLuhan\nIdea: We notice the content carried by a medium and overlook the environment the medium creates. Look for the part of experience your conceptual map makes difficult to perceive.\nContext: McLuhan's distinction between media content and the transformations produced by media themselves.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260907@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260831T170000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260907T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260907T011200
SUMMARY:Become singular
DESCRIPTION:Søren Kierkegaard\nIdea: A crowd can relieve the individual of responsibility. Becoming a self requires standing personally before the demand one recognizes as true rather than borrowing conviction from numbers.\nContext: Kierkegaard's writings on 'the single individual' and the crowd.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260908@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260831T170000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260908T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260908T011200
SUMMARY:The wound can be a door
DESCRIPTION:Carl Jung\nIdea: What the conscious personality rejects may return as symptom, projection, or disturbance. The troublesome material can become an entrance into a larger self if it is encountered rather than merely expelled.\nContext: Jung's work on shadow and individuation.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260909@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260831T170000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260909T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260909T011200
SUMMARY:Usefulness has a shadow
DESCRIPTION:George MacDonald\nIdea: A thing need not justify itself by immediate utility. Imagination, story, play, and beauty may enlarge the person who later decides what counts as useful.\nContext: MacDonald's essays on imagination and fairy tales.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260910@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260831T170000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260910T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260910T011200
SUMMARY:Less can make more possible
DESCRIPTION:Buckminster Fuller\nIdea: Good design does not merely add resources. It can reorganize relationships so that less matter and energy support greater capability.\nContext: Fuller's idea of doing more with less and ephemeralization.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260911@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260831T170000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260911T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260911T011200
SUMMARY:Knowing can unmake wonder
DESCRIPTION:Gregory of Nyssa\nIdea: In the divine life, genuine knowledge does not terminate desire. Encounter enlarges the capacity for further encounter; fulfillment can deepen rather than extinguish longing.\nContext: Gregory's account of endless progress, often called epektasis, especially in The Life of Moses.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260912@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260831T170000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260912T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260912T011200
SUMMARY:Remove one certainty
DESCRIPTION:Pseudo-Dionysius the Areopagite\nIdea: Negation can be intellectually productive. To say what ultimate reality is not can loosen concepts that have quietly become substitutes for what they attempt to describe.\nContext: The Mystical Theology and the apophatic tradition.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260913@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260831T170000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260913T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260913T011200
SUMMARY:What owns your attention?
DESCRIPTION:Evagrius Ponticus\nIdea: A recurring thought is not merely something you think. Repetition can give it a kind of agency over attention and action. Observe the thought's pattern before arguing with its content.\nContext: Evagrius's analysis of logismoi, recurring tempting or obsessive thought-patterns.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260914@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260831T170000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260914T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260914T011200
SUMMARY:A cell can teach
DESCRIPTION:Desert Fathers, especially Abba Moses and the sayings tradition\nIdea: Remaining in one place can expose evasions that movement disguises. Constraint sometimes becomes an instrument of perception.\nContext: The Sayings of the Desert Fathers repeatedly associate staying in one's cell with learning and transformation.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260915@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260831T170000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260915T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260915T011200
SUMMARY:Love alters perception
DESCRIPTION:Simone Weil\nIdea: Attention is not neutral inspection. The quality of attention can make another person's reality perceptible without immediately converting that person into a project, category, or instrument.\nContext: Weil's writings on attention, affliction, and love of neighbor.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260916@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260831T170000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260916T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260916T011200
SUMMARY:The ordinary has depth
DESCRIPTION:Julian of Norwich\nIdea: A small, vulnerable thing can disclose an unexpectedly large metaphysical horizon. Scale and significance need not correspond.\nContext: Julian's vision of a small object 'the quantity of a hazelnut' held in her hand, used to contemplate creation and divine love.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260917@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260831T170000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260917T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260917T011200
SUMMARY:Unknow what you know
DESCRIPTION:Meister Eckhart\nIdea: Spiritual and intellectual possession can become an obstacle. Detachment includes relinquishing cherished images of truth when those images prevent encounter with what exceeds them.\nContext: Eckhart's sermons on detachment, poverty of spirit, and knowing God beyond images.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260918@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260831T170000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260918T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260918T011200
SUMMARY:Opposites can coincide
DESCRIPTION:Nicholas of Cusa\nIdea: At the limits of ordinary comparison, oppositions generated by finite concepts may cease to behave as oppositions. Contradiction can sometimes mark the boundary of a conceptual instrument rather than the boundary of reality.\nContext: Cusa's 'coincidence of opposites' and learned ignorance.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260919@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260831T170000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260919T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260919T011200
SUMMARY:Beauty can demand labor
DESCRIPTION:Dante Gabriel Rossetti\nIdea: Beauty need not be an escape from difficult making. The finished image can conceal revision, discipline, failure, and return; enchantment and workmanship are not opposites.\nContext: Rossetti's intertwined practice of poetry, painting, revision, and reworking.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260920@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260831T170000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260920T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260920T011200
SUMMARY:Renunciation makes room
DESCRIPTION:Christina Rossetti\nIdea: Refusal is not always mere deprivation. Giving something up can create a form of interior space in which another allegiance, perception, or desire becomes possible.\nContext: A recurring structure in Rossetti's devotional poetry, where desire, restraint, loss, and transformed desire interact.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:20260921@4am-wisdom
DTSTAMP:20260831T170000Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260921T011100
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20260921T011200
SUMMARY:Is the joke still a joke?
DESCRIPTION:Andy Kaufman\nIdea: A performance can destabilize the boundary between event and representation. When the audience cannot locate the frame, its own expectations become part of the material.\nContext: Kaufman's deliberate blurring of character, prank, performance, sincerity, and audience complicity.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
